# Working on the @@NAME@@ robot (R3 project)

This repository describes one robot. `robot.yaml` (schema `r3/robot@1`) is the
single source of truth for components, poses, harnesses, PCBs, software
bindings, requirements and simulation scenarios. Everything under `build/` is
generated and must never be edited or committed.

## Loop

1. Read the current state: `r3 status --json` (MCP: `r3_status`).
2. Read the spec with its revision: MCP `r3_get_spec` returns `sha256`.
3. Edit the spec. Prefer MCP `r3_patch_spec` with `expected_sha256`; a
   `spec_conflict` means another agent or human changed `robot.yaml`. Re-read
   and re-apply your change; never overwrite blindly. Direct file edits are
   fine for humans; keep comments and formatting.
4. Validate: `r3 validate --json` (MCP: `r3_validate`). Fast inner loop:
   `r3 validate --stage model`.
5. Diagnose: `r3 diagnose` groups errors by subject with hints. Diagnostics have
   stable `code`s, `subjects` (component, wire, binding ids) and numeric `data`.
   Fix the design, not the check. If a check is wrong, say why in the PR.
6. Simulate: `r3 sim run [--scenario <id>]` and inspect `build/sim/<id>/`.
7. Commit `robot.yaml`, sources and `r3.lock.yaml` changes with a message that
   names the diagnostics you fixed.

## Layout

- `robot.yaml`: the robot. Units are SI or explicit strings such as `25 mm`.
  Robot frame: +x forward, +y left, +z up, origin on the ground below the rear axle.
- `r3.lock.yaml`: pinned external revisions (platform, Onshape microversion, PCB hashes).
  Update it through the tools, not by hand.
- `catalog/`: project-local component, connector and wire additions
  (`components/*.yaml`, `connectors.yaml`, `wires.yaml`), merged over the platform catalog.
  Record provenance (`datasheet`, `measured`, `estimated`).
- `cad/`: CAD generator source and part mapping (Onshape).
- `pcb/<board>/`: KiCad generator inputs and the generated KiCad project.
- `backend/`: robot software in the BLITZ layout, `backend/<language>/<module>/`.
  Software uses only `r3_hal`, so the same module runs on the robot
  (`R3_HAL=linux`) and against the digital twin (`R3_HAL=virtual`).
  `backend/deploy.py` keeps the hooks `get_modules()` and `pi_name_to_process_types()`.

## Permissions

- Free: reading, validating, simulating, editing the spec and sources.
- Ask the human first and pass explicit confirmation: `r3 deploy --target fleet --yes`
  (MCP `deploy_fleet(confirm=true)`) changes software on physical robots;
  `create_github_repo(confirm=true)` creates an account-visible repository.
  Ordering PCBs or parts is never done by an agent.

## Rules

- Do not hand-edit generated files (`build/`, generated KiCad projects).
- Distinguish datasheet values from estimates in the catalog and in PR text.
- A passing validation is not proof the robot works: read the `assumptions`
  in `build/report/summary.json` before claiming success.
